WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:06.364
At sunset Dr. Losberne entered the parlor, where Oliver and Mrs. Maylie had been sitting for hours.

2
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:12.451
"Tell me at once!" cried Mrs. Maylie, rising from the couch. "Is Rose dead?"

3
00:00:12.452 --> 00:00:20.443
"Compose yourself, dear ma'am," said the doctor. "Rose has survived, and she will live to bless us all for years to come."

4
00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:27.435
The good news was almost too much to bear. Mrs. Maylie sank with relief into Dr. Losberne's arms.

5
00:00:27.436 --> 00:00:34.189
Oliver was stunned. He could not weep or speak or remain in that room for one moment longer.

6
00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:40.919
He went out for a long, rambling walk in the evening air. At last he burst into tears of relief.

7
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:50.348
Wiping his eyes, he began to gather flowers for Rose. The night was closing in as he headed homeward, his arms laden with a huge bouquet.

8
00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:55.014
As he walked along the road, he heard the sound of a carriage approaching from behind.

9
00:00:55.015 --> 00:01:00.824
It dashed past him, and he caught a glimpse of a familiar face. Mr. Giles!

10
00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:04.744
The carriage came to a halt, and the butler stuck his head out the window.

11
00:01:04.745 --> 00:01:09.569
"Oliver!" cried Mr. Giles. "How is Miss Rose?"

12
00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:18.244
Oliver ran up to the coach. A young gentleman pushed Mr. Giles aside and addressed Oliver. "Is she better or worse?"

13
00:01:18.245 --> 00:01:22.482
"Better. Much better, sir!" said Oliver.

14
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:27.720
"Are you sure?" The gentleman jumped out of the carriage and took Oliver by the arm.

15
00:01:27.721 --> 00:01:33.061
"I'm quite sure, sir. Dr. Losberne says that she's out of danger."

16
00:01:33.062 --> 00:01:37.815
The gentleman turned his face away and remained silent for some moments.

17
00:01:37.816 --> 00:01:45.799
Oliver thought he heard the man sobbing while Mr. Giles sat on the carriage steps, wiping his eyes with a blue handkerchief dotted with white spots.

18
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:52.958
"You go ahead to my mother's house, Giles," said the young man. "I'd rather walk to gain a little time before I see her."

19
00:01:52.959 --> 00:02:00.624
"I beg your pardon, Mr. Harry, but I'd like to walk with you." Mr. Giles gave his face one final wipe with the handkerchief.

20
00:02:00.625 --> 00:02:07.267
"It wouldn't be proper for the staff to see me in this state. I'd never have any authority over them if they did."

21
00:02:07.268 --> 00:02:14.529
Harry Maylie smiled. "You may do as you like." So the three of them walked slowly toward the cottage.

22
00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:18.267
Mrs. Maylie was anxiously waiting for her son when he arrived.

23
00:02:18.268 --> 00:02:24.434
"Mother!" said the young man as they embraced. "Why didn't you write to me sooner?"

24
00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:30.664
"I did. But then I decided not to send the letter until Dr. Losberne had seen Rose."

25
00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:35.926
"But what if she had died?" chided Harry. "You know I love her!"

26
00:02:35.927 --> 00:02:45.310
"I know that she deserves the best love that a man can offer. And now I must say something that it pains me to say." Mrs. Maylie paused.

27
00:02:45.311 --> 00:02:48.744
"Sometimes a young man's love doesn't last."

28
00:02:48.744 --> 00:02:50.815
"That is unkind, Mother."

29
00:02:50.816 --> 00:02:59.474
"Rose has a stain upon her name, Harry. She's illegitimate. And you're ambitious, which means people will sneer at her."

30
00:02:59.475 --> 00:03:09.799
Mrs. Maylie fixed her eyes on her son's face. "If you marry her, you may live to regret your decision. And seeing your regret will only hurt Rose."

31
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.315
"Are you going to oppose my marriage?" asked Harry.

32
00:03:13.316 --> 00:03:16.974
"I won't, but I'd like you to consider—"

33
00:03:16.975 --> 00:03:21.720
"I have considered these things for years and years," Harry said impatiently.

34
00:03:21.721 --> 00:03:30.712
"My feelings remain unchanged, and they always will. There is no point in remaining silent. Before I leave this place, Rose will hear me."

35
00:03:30.713 --> 00:03:34.863
"Yes, she will hear you," Mrs. Maylie echoed flatly.

36
00:03:34.864 --> 00:03:42.284
"You sound as if she will hear my offer of marriage but not accept me. Has she fallen in love with someone else?" Harry demanded.

37
00:03:42.285 --> 00:03:49.355
"No indeed. You have a strong hold on her affections. But think of her history.

38
00:03:49.356 --> 00:03:57.347
Her awareness of her situation may influence her decision. Devoted as she is to us, she can be self-sacrificing."

39
00:03:57.348 --> 00:03:59.180
"What do you mean?"

40
00:03:59.181 --> 00:04:05.426
"I leave that to you to discover," replied Mrs. Maylie. "I must go back to her now."

41
00:04:05.427 --> 00:04:09.101
"You will tell her I am here?" said Harry.

42
00:04:09.102 --> 00:04:10.767
"Of course."

43
00:04:10.768 --> 00:04:16.569
"And say how anxious I have been and how I long to see her? You will not refuse to do this, Mother?"

44
00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:23.228
"No, I will tell her everything." Pressing her son's hand affectionately, Mrs. Maylie left the room.

45
00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:29.926
Harry was soon able to see Rose for himself. But he knew that now was not the time to talk of marriage.

46
00:04:29.927 --> 00:04:36.903
That would have to wait until she was better. In the meantime he went out every morning with Oliver to pick flowers for her.

47
00:04:36.904 --> 00:04:44.823
The days flew by, and Rose was rapidly recovering. She was still confined to her bed, so there were no evening walks.

48
00:04:44.824 --> 00:04:51.394
But Oliver was not idle. He applied himself to his studies and surprised himself with how much he learned.

49
00:04:51.395 --> 00:04:56.212
The little room where he studied was on the ground floor at the back of the house.

50
00:04:56.213 --> 00:05:01.291
The window overlooked a garden, and beyond that lay fine meadowland and woods.

51
00:05:01.292 --> 00:05:11.618
One warm evening at twilight, Oliver sat reading in this room. He had been poring over his books for some time and began to grow drowsy. Gradually he fell asleep.

52
00:05:11.619 --> 00:05:19.451
There is a kind of sleep in which the person is still conscious of sounds and things going on around him. That is the state that Oliver was in.

53
00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:27.102
He knew perfectly well that he was in his own little room and that his books were lying on the table before him. And yet he was asleep.

54
00:05:27.103 --> 00:05:35.269
Suddenly the scene changed, and the air became dank and heavy. He thought, with a shudder of terror, that he was in Fagin's house again.

55
00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:41.618
The hideous old man sat in his usual corner of the kitchen, pointing at Oliver and whispering to another man.

56
00:05:41.619 --> 00:05:49.832
Oliver thought he heard Fagin say, "Hush, my dear! It's the boy, sure enough. Come away."

57
00:05:49.833 --> 00:05:55.840
"That boy!" the other man seemed to answer. "I'd recognize him anywhere."

58
00:05:55.841 --> 00:06:02.015
The man seemed to say this with such dreadful hatred that Oliver woke up. He couldn't move.

59
00:06:02.016 --> 00:06:06.919
He couldn't speak because there—right there at the window—stood Fagin!

60
00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.657
Fagin was so close that Oliver could have almost touched him.

61
00:06:10.658 --> 00:06:22.332
And standing beside him, white with fear or rage or both, was the man from the inn—the angry man in the dark cloak who had threatened Oliver! A red scar marred the man's neck.

62
00:06:22.333 --> 00:06:30.467
The two men were at the window for only an instant, and then they were gone. But they had recognized Oliver, and he had recognized them.

63
00:06:30.468 --> 00:06:39.667
Oliver stood transfixed for a moment. Then, leaping from the window into the garden, he called loudly for help.

